selector_to_html = {"a[href=\"#submodules\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Submodules<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#submodules\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>", "a[href=\"duty/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.turbine.duty<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.turbine.duty\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>The duty a turbine is designed for: a gas expanding between two pressures.</p><p>Unlike a pump\u2019s, a turbine\u2019s duty cannot be given as head and flow alone.\nThe gas expands on its way through, so the volumetric flow at the exit can be\na good deal larger than at the inlet, and every blade height downstream is\nsized on that. <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"duty/TurbineDuty.html#pyskyfire.turbine.duty.TurbineDuty\" title=\"pyskyfire.turbine.duty.TurbineDuty\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">TurbineDuty</span></code></a> therefore always carries the states: a\nmedium, a mass flow, the inlet state and the pressure the gas leaves at.</p>", "a[href=\"axial/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.turbine.axial<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.turbine.axial\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Meanline design of an axial turbine, from its duty to a <code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">TurbineDefinition</span></code>.</p><p>The design runs at mean radius, which is the same at every station, so blade\nspeed is too. Each stage is a stator and a rotor, and for each one you choose:</p>", "a[href=\"_pyturbo/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.turbine._pyturbo<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.turbine._pyturbo\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Turbine blade sections from pyTurbo 1.3.8, wrapped onto the annulus.</p><p>pyTurbo\u2019s <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Airfoil2D</span></code> draws a cascade section flowing top to bottom (-y),\nwith the trailing edge at the origin and angles from axial. It turns the flow\ntoward +x. This package\u2019s angles are positive in the rotation direction, and\na row can turn the flow either way (a stator towards the rotation, a rotor\nback against it), so each section is drawn turning the pyTurbo way and\nmirrored where the row turns the other way.</p>", "a[href=\"#module-pyskyfire.turbine\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.turbine<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.turbine\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Axial turbine design: the duty, a meanline design, and the hardware it produces.</p>", "a[href=\"definition/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.turbine.definition<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.turbine.definition\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>The drawing of an axial turbine\u2019s bladed flow path.</p><p>A <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"definition/TurbineDefinition.html#pyskyfire.turbine.definition.TurbineDefinition\" title=\"pyskyfire.turbine.definition.TurbineDefinition\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">TurbineDefinition</span></code></a> is hardware and nothing else: an ordered tuple of\n<a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"definition/BladeRow.html#pyskyfire.turbine.definition.BladeRow\" title=\"pyskyfire.turbine.definition.BladeRow\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">BladeRow</span></code></a> objects and the annular passage they sit in. It holds no\nvelocity triangles, no duty and no efficiency, so it does not care how it was\ndesigned. A meanline design produces one, a user can write one out by hand,\nand evaluators and the geometry builder read one.</p>", "a[href=\"geometry/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.turbine.geometry<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.turbine.geometry\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Built turbine blades, and what can only be measured on them.</p><p>A <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"geometry/TurbineGeometry.html#pyskyfire.turbine.geometry.TurbineGeometry\" title=\"pyskyfire.turbine.geometry.TurbineGeometry\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">TurbineGeometry</span></code></a> is what <code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">TurbineDefinition.build_geometry()</span></code>\nreturns: one <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"geometry/RowGeometry.html#pyskyfire.turbine.geometry.RowGeometry\" title=\"pyskyfire.turbine.geometry.RowGeometry\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">RowGeometry</span></code></a> per row, and the passage they sit in. Each\nrow carries one blade\u2019s surfaces, which the scene repeats round the wheel,\nand the numbers the drawing left to the built section:</p>"}
skip_classes = ["headerlink", "sd-stretched-link"]

window.onload = function () {
    for (const [select, tip_html] of Object.entries(selector_to_html)) {
        const links = document.querySelectorAll(` ${select}`);
        for (const link of links) {
            if (skip_classes.some(c => link.classList.contains(c))) {
                continue;
            }

            tippy(link, {
                content: tip_html,
                allowHTML: true,
                arrow: true,
                placement: 'auto-start', maxWidth: 600, interactive: false,
                onShow(instance) {MathJax.typesetPromise([instance.popper]).then(() => {});},
            });
        };
    };
    console.log("tippy tips loaded!");
};
